<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<worksheet xml:id="activity-exponential-logarithm-equations">
  <title>Solving Exponential and Logarithm Equations</title>

  <objectives xml:id="obj-exponential-logarithm-equations">
    <ul>
      <li><p>Solve equations with an unknown exponent by isolating the exponential expression and applying <m>\ln</m> to both sides, using <m>e^u=N \Rightarrow u=\ln(N)</m> and <m>b^u=c \Rightarrow u\ln(b)=\ln(c)</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Determine the constants in an exponential model <m>y=Ae^{r(t-t_0)}</m> from two data points, rewrite it in the form <m>y=Ce^{rt}</m>, and interpret doubling time and half-life.</p></li>
      <li><p>Determine the exponent in a power law model <m>y=A(x/x_0)^p</m> from two data points, and distinguish a power law from an exponential model.</p></li>
      <li><p>Rewrite any power <m>u^w</m> with <m>u\gt 0</m> as <m>e^{w\ln(u)}</m>, and use this form to express a power with a different base in terms of <m>e</m>.</p></li>
      <li><p>Interpret <m>\log_b(c)</m> as the exponent <m>u</m> with <m>b^u=c</m>, evaluate simple logarithms such as <m>\log_3(27)</m> and <m>\log_2(1/16)</m> without a calculator, and use <m>\log_b(c)=\dfrac{\ln(c)}{\ln(b)}</m> to evaluate the rest.</p></li>
      <li><p>Solve equations involving logarithms, checking the domain of each solution.</p></li>
    </ul>
  </objectives>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      Now that we have both <m>\ln(x)</m> and <m>e^x</m>, and we know they are inverse functions,
      we can solve equations where the unknown sits in an exponent. This is the key skill
      for fitting exponential and power law models to data. The same idea also lets us
      write every power in terms of <m>e</m>, and every logarithm in terms of <m>\ln</m>, so
      that the natural exponential and natural logarithm are all we need for any base.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-solve-for-exponent" workspace="0in">
    <title>Solving for an Unknown Exponent</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Recall from <xref ref="def-e-and-exponentials"/> that <m>e^u=N</m> means exactly that
        <m>\ln(N)=u</m>, and that <m>\ln(b^u)=u\ln(b)</m>. Because <m>\ln</m> is one-to-one, applying
        it to both sides of an equation never creates or loses a solution, as long as both sides are positive.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-solve-natural-exponential" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve <m>e^{3x-1}=5</m> for <m>x</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-solve-isolate-first" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve <m>4e^{-x/2}+1=13</m> for <m>x</m>. What must you do before applying <m>\ln</m>, and why?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-solve-other-base" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve <m>2^x=10</m> for <m>x</m> by applying <m>\ln</m> to both sides. Which property of
          the logarithm moves the unknown out of the exponent?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-solve-other-base-coefficient" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve <m>5\cdot 3^{2t}=40</m> for <m>t</m>. Write your exact answer using only <m>\ln(2)</m> and <m>\ln(3)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-solve-no-solution" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Explain why <m>e^x=-2</m> has no solution. What does this say about the range of <m>e^x</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-solve-error-analysis" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A student solves <m>3+2^x=11</m> by <q>taking the logarithm of both sides</q> and writing
          <m>\ln(3)+x\ln(2)=\ln(11)</m>. Explain what went wrong. Then solve the equation correctly.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-exponential-models" workspace="0in">
    <title>Fitting an Exponential Model to Two Data Points</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        An exponential model can be written <m>y=Ae^{r(t-t_0)}</m>. When <m>t=t_0</m>, the exponent
        is zero and <m>y=A</m>, so if one of our data points is <m>(t_0,A)</m>, the constant <m>A</m>
        is already known. Only the rate <m>r</m> has to be found, and that is an equation with an unknown exponent.
      </p>
      <p>
        A culture of bacteria is observed to have 400 cells at time <m>t=2</m> hours and 1500 cells at
        time <m>t=5</m> hours.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-model-set-up" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Which data point should play the role of <m>(t_0,A)</m>? Write the model with this choice,
          leaving only <m>r</m> unknown.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-model-find-rate" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Substitute the other data point and solve for <m>r</m>. Is <m>r</m> positive or negative?
          Does that match the data?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-model-doubling-time" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          How long does it take for the population to double? Set up the equation
          <m>Ae^{r\,T}=2A</m>, solve for <m>T</m> in terms of <m>r</m>, and then evaluate. Does your answer depend on
          the starting size <m>A</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-model-rewrite" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Rewrite your model in the form <m>y=Ce^{rt}</m> by using <m>e^{a+b}=e^a e^b</m>.
          What is <m>C</m> in terms of <m>A</m>, <m>r</m>, and <m>t_0</m>, and what does it mean in the context of the culture?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-model-decay" workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          A drug concentration is measured as 12 mg/L at <m>t=1</m> hour and 3 mg/L at <m>t=4</m> hours.
          Build a model <m>y=Ae^{r(t-t_0)}</m>, find <m>r</m> exactly, and explain the sign of <m>r</m>.
          Then find the half-life, the time it takes for the concentration to drop to half of any given value.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-power-law-models" workspace="0in">
    <title>Fitting a Power Law Model</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        A power law model can be written <m>y=A\left(\dfrac{x}{x_0}\right)^p</m>. Again, when <m>x=x_0</m>
        we get <m>y=A</m>, so one data point <m>(x_0,A)</m> gives the constant, and the other gives an equation
        for the exponent <m>p</m>.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-power-kepler" workspace="1.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The Earth is <m>1</m> astronomical unit (AU) from the Sun and has an orbital period of <m>1</m> year.
          Mars is <m>1.524</m> AU from the Sun and has an orbital period of <m>1.881</m> years. Take
          <m>(x_0,A)=(1,1)</m> to model the period <m>T</m> as a power law in the distance <m>a</m>:
          <m>T=\left(\dfrac{a}{1}\right)^p</m>. Solve for <m>p</m>. What simple number is your answer close to?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-power-metabolic" workspace="1.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Suppose an animal of mass 4 kg uses energy at a rate of 14 W, and an animal of mass 64 kg uses
          energy at a rate of 112 W. Model the rate <m>R</m> as <m>R=14\left(\dfrac{m}{4}\right)^p</m>
          and find <m>p</m> exactly. Your answer should simplify to a rational number. Which property of
          logarithms makes that happen?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-power-as-exponential" workspace="0in">
    <title>Every Power Is a Natural Exponential</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>
        By the definition of real powers, <m>\ln(u^w)=w\ln(u)</m> for <m>u\gt 0</m>. Reading this
        as an exponential equation gives
        <md>u^w = e^{w\ln(u)} \qquad (u\gt 0)\text{.}</md>
        This lets us treat any power, including ones where both the base and the exponent vary, as a
        natural exponential.
      </p>
    </introduction>
    <task xml:id="task-power-rewrite" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Rewrite each as a natural exponential <m>e^{(\cdots)}</m>: <m>5^x</m>, <m>x^\pi</m>, and <m>x^x</m>.
          For which values of <m>x</m> is each rewriting valid?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-power-rewrite-drug" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          The drug concentration from before can be written <m>y=12\left(\tfrac{1}{2}\right)^{2(t-1)/3}</m>.
          Rewrite it using base <m>e</m>. How does your exponent compare with your model from
          <xref ref="task-model-decay"/>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <definition xml:id="def-log-base-b">
    <title>Logarithm with Base <m>b</m></title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>b\gt 0</m> with <m>b\ne 1</m>, and let <m>c\gt 0</m>. The <term>logarithm with base <m>b</m></term>
        of <m>c</m>, written <m>\log_b(c)</m>, is the exponent <m>u</m> that solves <m>b^u=c</m>:
        <md>\log_b(c)=u \quad \Leftrightarrow \quad b^u=c\text{.}</md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-log-as-exponent" workspace="0in">
    <title>A Logarithm Is an Exponent</title>
    <task xml:id="task-logb-evaluate" workspace="1.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Without a calculator, find <m>\log_3(27)</m> and <m>\log_2\left(\frac{1}{16}\right)</m>.
          For each, write the equation <m>b^u=c</m> that you are solving.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-logb-evaluate-more" workspace="1.75in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find each of <m>\log_5(1)</m>, <m>\log_{10}(0.001)</m>, <m>\log_4(2)</m>, and
          <m>\log_{1/2}(8)</m>. Which of these are negative, and why does the base or the input tell you so
          in advance?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-logb-inverse" workspace="1.25in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the definition to explain why <m>\log_b(b^p)=p</m> for every real <m>p</m> and
          <m>b^{\log_b(x)}=x</m> for every <m>x\gt 0</m>. What does this say about the functions
          <m>b^x</m> and <m>\log_b(x)</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-logb-not-a-power" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Between which two integers is <m>\log_2(10)</m>? It is not a nice exponent, so
          solve <m>2^u=10</m> using the method from <xref ref="ex-solve-for-exponent"/> and write <m>u</m> in
          terms of <m>\ln</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-logb-general-formula" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Repeat the previous task for a general equation <m>b^u=c</m> to find a formula for
          <m>\log_b(c)</m> in terms of natural logarithms.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <theorem xml:id="thm-change-of-base">
    <title>Change of Base</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        For <m>b\gt 0</m>, <m>b\ne 1</m>, and <m>c\gt 0</m>,
        <md>\log_b(c) = \frac{\ln(c)}{\ln(b)}\text{.}</md>
        In particular, <m>\ln(c)=\log_e(c)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-solve-log-equations" workspace="0in">
    <title>Solving Equations with Logarithms</title>
    <task xml:id="task-logb-properties" workspace="1in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Use the change of base formula and <xref ref="thm-ln-algebraic-properties"/> to explain why
          <m>\log_b(xy)=\log_b(x)+\log_b(y)</m>. Which other properties do you expect <m>\log_b</m> to share?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-logb-solve-log-equation" workspace="1.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve <m>\ln(2x+1)=3</m> and <m>\log_2(x-1)=3</m>. What do you do to both sides of each
          equation to undo the logarithm?
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
    <task xml:id="task-logb-solve-combine" workspace="2in">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve <m>\log_2(x)+\log_2(x-2)=3</m>. Combine the logarithms first, and then check each
          candidate solution against the domain of the original equation.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <assemblage xml:id="assemblage-exp-log-equations-summary">
    <title>Summary</title>
    <p>
      To solve for an unknown exponent, isolate the exponential expression, then apply <m>\ln</m>:
      <md>
        <mrow> e^u = N \amp \Rightarrow u = \ln(N) </mrow>
        <mrow> b^u = c \amp \Rightarrow u\ln(b) = \ln(c) \ \Rightarrow\ u = \log_b(c) </mrow>
      </md>
      Every power with a positive base is a natural exponential, <m>u^w=e^{w\ln(u)}</m>, and every
      logarithm is an exponent, <m>\log_b(c)=u \Leftrightarrow b^u=c</m>, which can be computed from natural
      logarithms as <m>\log_b(c)=\ln(c)/\ln(b)</m>. When solving
      an equation with logarithms, undo the logarithm with the matching exponential, and always check
      that each solution lies in the domain of the original equation.
    </p>
  </assemblage>
</worksheet>
